{"kind":"request","text":"Give every durable event a sequence number, make `#N` the primary way people and agents name items, and stop the interfaces from displaying truncated hashes.\n\nThe README already promises this as the first of three rungs, at README.md:69-72: \"Every event gets a ticket number. Signed at the door, final, the same number for every reader. 'As of #4312' means one exact thing, forever.\" Rungs two and three speak the same way — \"Rests on #4290\", \"Replaces #4101\", \"Projected from #4312\". Nothing in the system delivers it. A projected statement carries event, actor, kind, text, body and timestamp, where `event` is the full `git:sha1:\u003cgenesis\u003e#git:sha1:\u003c40-hex\u003e`. There is no number, index or ordinal in the projection, in `gs status --json`, or in any MCP response, and SKILL.md:165 instructs agents the other way: \"Always use the canonical full event ID.\"\n\nHugh has decided the direction, so it is not an open question here: sequence numbers become the primary way of talking about items, always written with a leading `#`, with the canonical full identifier retained for any case where a short number could be ambiguous.\n\nThe UI is not precedent for this, it is the biggest instance of the problem. ui/src/lib/api.ts:327-331 defines `shortEvent` as `bare.slice(0, 8)` and `shortHash` beside it does the same. Those eight characters look like a name and are not one: they cannot be resolved without a lookup, and every surface that prints them teaches readers to speak in a form that does not round-trip. The `sequence` field already in that file belongs to ephemeral frames — `conversation:sequence` — and has nothing to do with durable events.\n\nThe position already exists in the fold. internal/workroom/fold.go carries an `index` per parsed record; it is how the founding-seed rule works, `record.index == 0`. This is surfacing something already computed.\n\nWhy this is worth doing beyond matching the README. A 40-hex identifier has no redundancy: every character is equally plausible, so a wrong one is indistinguishable from a right one until the fold rejects it. Three times in one session I read an eight-character prefix off a listing and wrote a plausible remainder. Each act appended cleanly, reported success, and carried no force. One left a completed review invisible for twenty minutes; one would have put an unresolvable citation into an immutable commit trailer. A short ordinal cannot be confabulated the same way, because inventing one is noticeable, and it can be checked by eye against a listing.\n\nTwo constraints, because getting them wrong makes things worse. The number means something only within one genesis, so anything crossing workrooms needs the canonical form and the surfaces must make that boundary visible rather than leaving it to be discovered. And it should be a display and lookup affordance rather than a second identifier the fold accepts in a citation: two accepted names for one event is a trap of its own, and the fold's flat refusal to resolve a wrong identifier is a property worth keeping.","body":{"conditions":"Every projected durable event carries its sequence position, derived from the fold's existing per-record index rather than newly assigned, and stable for a given genesis and event across a re-fold of the same log, with a test pinning that stability; the number is exposed through the projection, `gs status --json` and the MCP surface, so an agent can read `#N` without a lookup; the UI names durable events by `#N` and no longer displays a truncated hash as if it were a name — `shortEvent` and `shortHash` in ui/src/lib/api.ts are removed or reduced to places where a git object, not an event, is being shown; the CLI's human-readable output does the same; documentation and SKILL.md are brought into agreement with the implementation, telling agents to name items as `#N` within a workroom and to use the canonical full identifier where a workroom is ambiguous or the reference crosses one; the surfaces make the single-genesis boundary visible rather than implicit; the fold's citation resolution is unchanged, since a second accepted name for one event is a separate decision that would need its own record; `make test`, `go vet` and the UI gates pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}